                S3(A("CONS"), S4(A("COMP"), A("length"), N(2), N(1)),
                   S3(A("COMP"), N(1), N(1)))))),
       S3(A("COMP"), A("apndl"),
          S3(A("CONS"),
             S3(A("COMP"), A("apply"),
                S3(A("CONS"), S3(A("COMP"), N(1), N(1)),
                   S3(A("COMP"), N(2), N(1)))),
             N(2))),
       N(2))),

DEF("rmap:atpos",
    S4(A("COMP"), S2(A("INSERT"), A("rmap:ap_keep")),
       A("theta:append_phi"), A("distl"))),

"rmap:valpairs is the functional column's <id,value> pairs, in ROW ORDER, from the rows long enough to have both. The unary column needs no DEF of its own: it is rmap:atpos at position 1, which is what atpos being a general projection buys. What is NOT here is the last-wins rule or the keying. engine/rust builds a HashMap and lets a later row overwrite an earlier one, keyed by key_of -- the store's value identity, which coalesces 5 with 5.0 while keeping \"5\" distinct. Expressing last-wins in canon means deciding whether canon's eq IS that identity, and it is not obviously so; emitting the pairs in row order leaves the host's map to settle it exactly as before, and leaves the question open where it belongs rather than answering it by accident.",
"rmap:colfts and rmap:coltables are the two projections of the rmapColumns rows <table,col,ft> that the rest needs: which fact types some table absorbed, and which tables absorb anything. Both are rmap:atpos with a constant position -- its THIRD reuse, after the id sweep and the unary column -- so neither needs a fold of its own. dedup's last-occurrence order does not matter here: one feeds setminus as an exclusion set, the other lands in a set. NOTE they carry only atpos's length guard, not engine/rust's additional demand that the column be an INTEGER; rmap:colsof already had that same gap and the op suite exercises the real store, so this is consistent rather than newly permissive -- a malformed rmapColumns row would be kept here where the Rust dropped it.",
"THE RULE TABLE's split, the first piece of op_run_rules to leave the host (engine/rust 4445). ruleDerives rows <rule id, head cell> partition on ruleAgg membership: an AGGREGATE head supersedes rather than unions, so the closure must never run one, and the two lists are what the semi-naive loop and the upper stratum each consume. derive:aggids is rmap:atpos at position 1 -- its FOURTH reuse, and a sign that atpos is misnamed under rmap: since it is a general projection; left where it is rather than churning every call site. NOTE the membership test is canon's theta:member (canon eq), where engine/rust compares key_of strings. Those agree for STRING rule ids, which is what a rule id is; they would part on 2 versus 2.0, the same value-identity question the projection increments keep arriving at. Stated, not assumed away.",
