"role_keep's guards, added after the fact. It reached elements 3 and 4 of a role row with NO length test, so a row shorter than four answered bottom and took the entire grouping with it -- not one row lost, the whole cell. It survived because the real store's role rows are all four wide, which is exactly the kind of luck that stops being luck. engine/rust's own loop over the same cell tests it.len() >= 4 AND the position >= 1; both are here now. NESTED CONDs, not `and`: `and` is strict and would evaluate the position test on a short row, which is the bottom the guard exists to avoid.",
DEF("rmap:role_keep",
    S4(A("COND"),
       S3(A("COMP"), A("ge"),
          S3(A("CONS"), S4(A("COMP"), A("length"), N(2), N(1)), K(N(4)))),
       S4(A("COND"),
          S3(A("COMP"), A("ge"),
             S3(A("CONS"), S4(A("COMP"), N(3), N(2), N(1)), K(N(1)))),
          S4(A("COND"),
             S3(A("COMP"), A("eq"),
                S3(A("CONS"), S4(A("COMP"), N(2), N(2), N(1)),
                   S3(A("COMP"), N(1), N(1)))),
             S3(A("COMP"), A("apndl"),
                S3(A("CONS"),
                   S3(A("CONS"), S4(A("COMP"), N(3), N(2), N(1)),
                      S4(A("COMP"), N(4), N(2), N(1))),
                   N(2))),
             N(2)),
          N(2)),
       N(2))),

DEF("rmap:rolesof",
    S4(A("COMP"), A("system:sort_asc"),
       S3(A("COMP"), S2(A("INSERT"), A("rmap:role_keep")), A("theta:append_phi")),
       A("distl"))),

"rmap:rolegroups is the whole `role` cell grouped in ONE reduction: <ft, <<pos,player>...>> for every fact type mentioned, the fact types in FIRST-SEEN order (theta:dedup keeps the first occurrence, which is the order engine/rust's role_ft_order kept and the entity sweep walks). Done as one call rather than rmap:rolesof per fact type deliberately -- the work is the same either way, but per-ft means a round trip per fact type and this store carries hundreds.",
"reverse . dedup . reverse, and the reverses are NOT ornament. theta:dedup keeps each value's LAST occurrence -- <F1,F2,F1> dedups to <F2,F1> -- while engine/rust's role_ft_order is FIRST-seen, which is the order the entity sweep walks. Deduping the reversed list turns last-occurrence into first-occurrence, and reversing back restores the original direction. The plain form was written first and the case caught it answering F2 before F1.",
"theta:firstseen is FIRST-occurrence dedup, which theta:dedup is not: dedup keeps each value's LAST occurrence, so <a,b,a> dedups to <b,a>. Deduping the reversed list turns last into first and reversing back restores the direction. rmap:role_fts had this inline and was the increment that discovered the behavior; engine/rust's id sweep needs the same rule (its id_seen set keeps the first id it meets), so it is named once here rather than written twice. Under theta: because nothing about it is RMAP's -- it is a sequence operation.",
"theta:aton is ntoa's INVERSE, and canon had one direction only. It is the function that ASSIGNS the ORM type: engine/rust's compile.rs num() decides whether '120' is an Integer or stays a String, and that type is precisely what canon's eq compares on (kernel.py: NATEQ, 'same ORM type + equal'). Having the number-to-text direction in canon and the text-to-number direction in Rust put the two halves of one decision on opposite sides of the boundary. The magnitude is a RIGHT fold carrying <value, weight>: INSERT folds from the right, which is where the units digit is, so weight starts at 1 and multiplies by 10 per step -- no positional index and no `iota` needed. FLOATS DO NOT COME HERE. Reproducing a platform's float parser in canon is the same mistake as reproducing its float formatter, which is what stopped set_key moving; a text that is not an optionally-signed run of digits answers ITSELF unchanged, and the host keeps the float branch.",
"theta:groupby1 is <k,v> rows to <k, <v...>> groups, keys in FIRST-SEEN order. rmap:rolegroups is this shape specialised to the role cell's 4-element rows; engine/rust's ruleReads map is the same shape again over 2-element rows, so it is named once here rather than written a third time. Under theta: because grouping a sequence by its first component is not RMAP's -- theta:firstseen, which it uses for the key order, is already there for the same reason. Rows shorter than 2 are SKIPPED, which is engine/rust's it.len() >= 2, and without that guard 2-of-a-1-element-row is bottom and takes the whole grouping with it.",
"derive:ftr_pairs builds the fact-type-reading rows from the role cell: each role row's <_, ft, ...> swapped to <ft, roleid>, DISTINCT, first occurrence kept. FIRST, not last -- engine/rust guards with a `seen` set that keeps what it met first, so theta:firstseen is the rule and theta:dedup would silently reverse which duplicate survives. Rows too short to name both are skipped, the same guard theta:gb_key_keep carries.",
"derive:uniq_constraints restricts the constraint cell to its UNIQUENESS kinds -- plain and spanning both -- and projects <constraint id, fact type>. The kind test is theta:member against a two-element list rather than two eq arms, so a third uniqueness kind is one atom rather than another branch. What stays host is the JOIN that follows: keyspans pairs these against the spans grouped separately, and joining two cells is the caller's business, not this restrict's.",
"derive:copy_pairs projects the ruleCopies cell's <_, antecedent, consequent> rows to the discharged inclusion pair. The host keeps the SET: these land in a HashSet and set semantics -- not order, not first-or-last -- is what that call site wants, so canon emits every pair in row order and the caller collapses them. Deduping here would decide a question the caller has already answered differently.",
"derive:txn_unambiguous is a FUNCTIONAL DEPENDENCY check, not a projection. A transition name defined in two different state machines does not identify a transition, so it is excluded WHOLE rather than resolved to either -- engine/rust reaches that by overwriting then popping, which is the same answer arrived at backwards. Names already carrying the txn: surrogate are skipped: they were keyed by an earlier pass and must not be re-derived. The bare test is strip_prefix against itself, the same shape rmap:ddl_name uses for sqlite_ -- if stripping the prefix changes nothing, the prefix was not there.",
"derive:reads_hit is the FRONTIER restriction, the first piece of the semi-naive loop to leave the host: a rule runs in round one only where the cells it reads INTERSECT the changed set. Empty either way answers F -- an empty frontier fires nothing, and a rule that reads nothing is never woken by a change, which is what engine/rust's any() over an empty iterator already said. Not the fixpoint itself: the rounds, the per-head deltas and the delta threading are still host, and derive:eval/delta_eval evaluate one BODY rather than driving the loop.",
"derive:atom_hits is derive:reads_hit's counterpart for the DELTA rounds: which of a rule's atoms <kind, fact type> name a fact type that gained rows last round. reads_hit answers WHETHER a rule wakes in round one; this answers WHICH atoms wake it afterwards, and the semi-naive join runs one ~d variant per hit. Order is preserved -- the join walks the hits in the rule's own atom order, and a fold that reversed them would reorder the variants it applies.",
DEF("derive:ah_keep",
    S4(A("COND"),
       S3(A("COMP"), A("theta:member"),
          S3(A("CONS"), S4(A("COMP"), N(2), N(2), N(1)),
             S3(A("COMP"), N(1), N(1)))),
