"AND THE SERVED SURFACE IS THE READABLE SCHEMA, NOT THE STORED ONE (2026-09-11). mcp:tools mapped over state:declared, which the oracle builds from the STORED schema: a fully derived (*) fact type leaves that schema by Codd 1970 1.5 -- strong redundancy as a stored relation -- so norma-oracle skips it on purpose (Verifier.cs, `fully derived fact types are strong redundancy as stored relations`) and writes it no descriptor. Its ROWS still exist: the closure computes them at boot and system:pop_rows answers them with no descriptor at all. On support.auto.dev that is 53 fact types, 47 of them with a reading and 11 carrying rows right now -- `Predicate is bound` (24), `Constraint awaits a decider` (12), the four Status-in-State-Machine-Definition heads -- and NONE of the 53 was offered as a tool. A verdict that is computed and unreachable is exactly what `verdicts are read, not stored` refuses. The stored schema stays what Codd says it is; the SURFACE is that plus the heads a rule delivers, which state:rules already carries as <name, players> in its first two fields. A store with no state:rules cell answers the stored schema alone rather than dying, the guard rules:model already carries for the same cell.",
