"AND THE SAME ENTRY AS SCHEMA, not only as a listed tool. state:declared is what mcp:tools flattens; state:fts is what store:fts reads, and a fact type absent from it is a tool nothing can hold a population for. The entry is <ftid, kinds, uniqueness, mandatory, population>: kinds are the generic Function the other 245 use, the population is empty, and the uniqueness is the one spanning ALL roles. That last is a choice and worth stating -- the reading declares no uniqueness, every one of the 245 existing fact types HAS one, and the all-roles constraint is the weakest expressible: it says a population is a set, which is already true, so it adds no claim the reading did not make. 40 fact types are already shaped that way. Arity above three refuses rather than guessing, since canon has no iota to build the role list with.",
DEF("compile:uc_of",
    S4(A("COND"),
       S3(A("COMP"), A("eq"), S3(A("CONS"), A("length"), K(N(1)))),
       K(S1(S1(N(1)))),
       S4(A("COND"),
          S3(A("COMP"), A("eq"), S3(A("CONS"), A("length"), K(N(2)))),
          K(S1(S2(N(1), N(2)))),
          S4(A("COND"),
             S3(A("COMP"), A("eq"), S3(A("CONS"), A("length"), K(N(3)))),
             K(S1(S3(N(1), N(2), N(3)))),
             K(PHI()))))),
DEF("compile:fts_of",
    S6(A("CONS"),
       A("main:event_ftid"),
       S3(A("COMP"), S2(A("ALPHA"), S2(A("CONST"), A("Function"))), A("compile:players_of")),
       S3(A("COMP"), A("compile:uc_of"), A("compile:players_of")),
       K(PHI()),
       S2(A("CONS"), K(PHI())))),
DEF("main:declare_fts_val",
    S3(A("COMP"), A("cat"),
       S3(A("CONS"),
          S3(A("COMP"), A("ast:fetch"), S3(A("CONS"), K(A("state:fts")), N(1))),
          S2(A("CONS"), S2(A("CONS"), S3(A("COMP"), A("compile:fts_of"), N(2))))))),
DEF("main:declare_fts",
    S3(A("COMP"), A("apply"),
       S3(A("CONS"),
          S3(A("COMP"), A("ast:Store"), K(A("state:fts"))),
          S3(A("CONS"), A("main:declare_fts_val"), N(1))))),
DEF("main:declare",
    S3(A("COMP"), A("main:declare_fts"),
       S3(A("CONS"), A("main:declare_dec"), N(2)))),
DEF("main:declare_dec",
    S3(A("COMP"), A("apply"),
       S3(A("CONS"),
          S3(A("COMP"), A("ast:Store"), K(A("state:declared"))),
          S3(A("CONS"), A("main:declare_val"), N(1))))),
"WHICH FACT TYPE ANSWERS AUTHORIZATION -- A DESIGNATION, NOT A SCHEMA. Canon must not hardcode apps/access's own fact type names: RBAC is one rule shape and ownership, tenancy and time-boxed grants are the same hook with a different rule, all of them derived populations reached by the same lfp, none of them a policy engine. So canon names the CELL where an app says which fact type answers <caller, operation, resource>, and the app populates it. This is the metamodel's own binding idiom -- `Derivation Rule is provided by Constraint` at core.md:949 binds one thing to another as an instance fact -- applied to authorization. THE READING IS THE APP'S (2026-09-09): the cell was named AuthorizationIsAnsweredByFactType after a reading no file declared, so no app could ever designate; `App answers authorization with Fact Type` (readings/templates/organizations.md, beside App's other facts) is the binding an app asserts -- `App 'support' answers authorization with Fact Type 'User is authorized'` -- and the app's auth context, in Sam's words, is exactly that fact.",
DEF("auth:designation",
    K(A("AppAnswersAuthorizationWithFactType"))),

"AN APP WITH NO AUTH CONTEXT PERMITS EVERY OPERATION (Sam, 2026-09-09: \"Users aren't necessary, but if an app has no auth context, then all operations are permitted. Permission is inherent to users.\"). OPEN is the default: an app that designates no fact type has no users to hold permissions and no gate to hold them to, so every link is offered to every caller, and the gate begins the moment the app asserts its designation. The earlier default was closed, on the argument that an app which forgot to designate would over-offer; that argument is overruled -- an app without users is not a forgotten gate, it is an app without one -- and the closed reading had in any case never let a single link through, since no reading declared the designation it waited for.",
DEF("auth:default_policy",
    K(A("open"))),

"THE DESIGNATION IS A BINARY READING, because that is what FORML writes. `App answers authorization with Fact Type.` gives a two-wide cell and the metamodel binds things the same way -- `Derivation Rule is provided by Constraint` at core.md:949 -- so canon reads element 2 of the first row rather than expecting a one-wide cell nobody would author. Canon fits the modeling language; the language does not bend to canon.",
DEF("auth:ft_of",
    S3(A("COMP"),
       S4(A("COND"), A("null"), S2(A("CONST"), PHI()),
          S3(A("COMP"), N(2), N(1))),
       S3(A("COMP"), A("apply"),
          S3(A("CONS"),
             S3(A("COMP"), A("apply"),
                S3(A("CONS"), K(A("system:ftpop_fetch")),
                   S3(A("CONS"), A("auth:designation"), K(N(2))))),
             A("id"))))),

"FORML TEXT TO THE ROWS read:parse READS -- the step that had no implementation left. read:row's note calls its input `tokenized sentence rows <indent, token-pairs>, each token a <original, Capitalized> pair -- tokenization and case are the lex boundary externalized`, and externalized meant the fat hosts did it. They are gone, so the fleet could compile a parse and not produce one. Nothing new is needed: lex:parts ALREADY tokenizes exactly as the witnesses do, splitting `(.Nr)` into ( . Nr ) where the registered lex leaves it whole, and cn:pascalw is already the capitaliser. What was missing was only their composition. The trailing period goes because a FORML statement ends in one and the witness rows do not carry it; a sentence that does not end in punctuation would lose a token, which is a grammar the fragment does not admit. MEASURED: 57 of the reader's 59 witness rows are reproduced from reconstructed text BYTE-IDENTICALLY. The two that are not are instance facts carrying QUOTED LITERALS -- 'OSI-1' must stay one token and lex:parts splits it into ' OSI 1 ' -- so populations do not yet round-trip. That is a gap in lex:parts, not in this composition, and it is the third time this session that a quoted span not treated atomically has broken something.",
"A QUOTED LITERAL IS ONE TOKEN, which the character scan has to know while it scans. lex:punctsplit drops what it does not recognize -- 'OSI-1' comes out as ' OSI 1 ' with the hyphen GONE -- so no amount of rejoining tokens afterwards can reconstruct the literal: the information is destroyed before tokens exist. This is a SECOND scanner rather than a quote flag threaded through the first, because every reading in every app flows through lex:punctsplit and only the text-to-rows path needs this. Additive beats surgical when the blast radius is the corpus.",
DEF("lex:q_alnum",
    S3(A("COMP"), A("theta:or"),
       S3(A("CONS"),
          S3(A("COMP"), A("eq"), S3(A("CONS"), A("charisup"), K(A("T")))),
          S3(A("COMP"), A("theta:or"),
             S3(A("CONS"),
                S3(A("COMP"), A("eq"), S3(A("CONS"), A("charislow"), K(A("T")))),
                S3(A("COMP"), A("eq"), S3(A("CONS"), A("charisdigit"), K(A("T"))))))))),

DEF("lex:q_flush",
    S4(A("COND"), S3(A("COMP"), A("null"), N(2)), N(1),
       S3(A("COMP"), A("apndr"), S3(A("CONS"), N(1), N(2))))),

"THE SCAN. State <out, run, chars, inquote>. A quote OPENS by flushing the run and starting a new one with the quote itself, and CLOSES by appending the quote and flushing -- so the delimiters stay in the token, which is what the corpus writes and what read:parse matches on. Inside a quote every character is taken verbatim, spaces and hyphens included, which is the whole point: 'Data Link' is one token and so is 'OSI-1'. Outside, it is lex:punctsplit's own rule -- alphanumerics accumulate, anything else flushes and stands alone.",
DEF("lex:qstep",
    S4(A("COND"),
       S3(A("COMP"), A("eq"), S3(A("CONS"), S3(A("COMP"), N(1), N(3)), K(A("'")))),
       S4(A("COND"),
          S3(A("COMP"), A("eq"), S3(A("CONS"), N(4), K(A("T")))),
          S5(A("CONS"),
             S3(A("COMP"), A("apndr"),
                S3(A("CONS"), N(1),
                   S3(A("COMP"), A("apndr"),
                      S3(A("CONS"), N(2), S3(A("COMP"), N(1), N(3)))))),
             K(PHI()), S3(A("COMP"), A("tl"), N(3)), K(A("F"))),
          S5(A("CONS"),
             S3(A("COMP"), A("lex:q_flush"), S3(A("CONS"), N(1), N(2))),
             S2(A("CONS"), S3(A("COMP"), N(1), N(3))),
             S3(A("COMP"), A("tl"), N(3)), K(A("T")))),
       S4(A("COND"),
          S3(A("COMP"), A("eq"), S3(A("CONS"), N(4), K(A("T")))),
          S5(A("CONS"), N(1),
             S3(A("COMP"), A("apndr"), S3(A("CONS"), N(2), S3(A("COMP"), N(1), N(3)))),
             S3(A("COMP"), A("tl"), N(3)), K(A("T"))),
          S4(A("COND"),
             S3(A("COMP"), A("lex:q_alnum"), S3(A("COMP"), N(1), N(3))),
             S5(A("CONS"), N(1),
                S3(A("COMP"), A("apndr"), S3(A("CONS"), N(2), S3(A("COMP"), N(1), N(3)))),
                S3(A("COMP"), A("tl"), N(3)), K(A("F"))),
             S5(A("CONS"),
                S3(A("COMP"), A("apndr"),
                   S3(A("CONS"),
                      S3(A("COMP"), A("lex:q_flush"), S3(A("CONS"), N(1), N(2))),
                      S2(A("CONS"), S3(A("COMP"), N(1), N(3))))),
                K(PHI()), S3(A("COMP"), A("tl"), N(3)), K(A("F"))))))),

DEF("lex:qsplit",
    S4(A("COMP"),
       S3(A("COMP"), A("lex:q_flush"), S3(A("CONS"), N(1), N(2))),
       S3(A("WHILE"), S4(A("COMP"), A("not"), A("null"), N(3)), A("lex:qstep")),
       S5(A("CONS"), K(PHI()), K(PHI()), A("chars"), K(A("F"))))),

"A SPACE OUTSIDE A QUOTE IS A SEPARATOR, NOT A TOKEN. The scan flushes on it like any non-alphanumeric, which would leave a bare space standing as its own token; INSIDE a quote it was already taken verbatim by the inquote arm, so dropping it here cannot touch 'Data Link'. Filtered after imploding rather than special-cased in the step, so the scan keeps one rule for everything that is not alphanumeric.",
DEF("lex:qparts",
    S3(A("COMP"), A("theta:flatten"),
       S3(A("COMP"),
          S2(A("ALPHA"),
             S4(A("COND"), S3(A("COMP"), A("eq"), S3(A("CONS"), A("id"), K(A(" ")))),
                K(PHI()), S2(A("CONS"), A("id")))),
          S3(A("COMP"),
             S2(A("ALPHA"), S3(A("COMP"), A("implode"), S3(A("CONS"), K(A("")), A("id")))),
             A("lex:qsplit"))))),

DEF("read:tokens_of",
    S3(A("COMP"), A("theta:butlast"),
       S3(A("COMP"),
          S2(A("ALPHA"),
             S4(A("COND"),
                S3(A("COMP"), A("eq"),
                   S3(A("CONS"), S3(A("COMP"), N(1), A("chars")), K(A("'")))),
                S3(A("CONS"), A("id"), A("id")),
                S3(A("CONS"), A("id"), A("cn:pascalw")))),
          A("lex:qparts")))),

DEF("read:row_of",
    S3(A("CONS"), K(N(0)), A("read:tokens_of"))),

"THE FOUR DERIVATION MARKERS, WHICH THE READER HAD NONE OF. NORMA's markers are * ** + ++ and system:derivation_modes has carried what each MEANS since long before this -- fully-derived, derived-and-stored, semi-derived, partially-derived-and-stored -- but read:parse recognized no derivation in any form, so the corpus's 1,131 leading and trailing markers were readable only by the hosts this branch deleted. They tokenize as single characters, so ** is two tokens and the marker is one or two of them.",
DEF("read:is_marker",
    S3(A("COMP"), A("theta:or"),
       S3(A("CONS"),
          S3(A("COMP"), A("eq"), S3(A("CONS"), A("id"), K(A("*")))),
          S3(A("COMP"), A("eq"), S3(A("CONS"), A("id"), K(A("+"))))))),

"THE LEADING MARKER OF A TOKEN LIST, or the empty sequence. One marker character or two; two of the SAME character, since * and + name different modes and *+ names none. Answering the marker as the string NORMA writes -- * ** + ++ -- is what lets system:derivation_modes stay the one place the meaning lives.",
DEF("read:marker_of",
    S4(A("COND"),
       S3(A("COMP"), A("not"), S3(A("COMP"), A("read:is_marker"), N(1))),
       K(PHI()),
       S4(A("COND"),
          S3(A("COMP"), A("eq"), S3(A("CONS"), N(1), N(2))),
          S3(A("COMP"), A("implode"),
             S3(A("CONS"), K(A("")), S3(A("CONS"), N(1), N(2)))),
          N(1)))),

"THE MARKER'S MEANING, from the one place it lives. system:derivation_modes is <marker, mode, materializes> and has carried NORMA's four since long before the reader could see one, so this looks the mode up rather than restating it -- a second table of the same four would be a second chance to disagree, which is the note on slug's whole argument.",
DEF("read:mode_of",
    S3(A("COMP"),
       S4(A("COND"), A("null"), S2(A("CONST"), PHI()), N(2)),
       S3(A("COMP"), A("theta:find_desc"),
          S3(A("CONS"), A("id"), A("system:derivation_modes"))))),

"A MARKED CONDITIONAL IS A DERIVATION, NOT A SUBSET. read:declares_subset is `declares_obligatory AND says_if`, so the ONE conditional the reader admitted was the deontic obligatory form -- a plain `X if Y` failed the first conjunct and produced no record at all, which is why 464 marked rules in the corpus were readable only by the deleted hosts. The marker is what separates the two: NORMA's * ** + ++ declare a DERIVATION over the head, where an unmarked conditional over an asserted head is a subset CHECK. Same sentence shape, different claim, and the marker says which.",
DEF("read:declares_derivation",
    S3(A("COMP"), A("and"),
       S3(A("CONS"),
          S3(A("COMP"), A("not"),
             S3(A("COMP"), A("null"),
                S3(A("COMP"), A("read:marker_of"), A("read:ring_words")))),
          A("read:says_if")))),

"THE TAG A DERIVATION CARRIES: <derivation, mode>, the mode read out of system:derivation_modes by the marker. compile:family_of_tag already routes `derivation`, and compile:builder already resolves every storage mode, so this is the last link -- the reader saying which of the four a rule head is.",
"THE BODY, RESOLVED. Each clause is an ordinary fact-type reading, so it is resolved by read:reading against the same declared names the head is -- distl carries the known-name set into every clause. That is what makes a rule's antecedent ATOMS rather than words: the clause names a fact type, and the compiler's job downstream is the join on shared role players.",
DEF("read:body_clauses",
    S3(A("COMP"),
       S2(A("ALPHA"), A("read:reading")),
       S3(A("COMP"), A("distl"),
          S3(A("CONS"), N(1),
             S3(A("COMP"), A("read:clauses"),
                S3(A("COMP"), N(2),
                   S3(A("COMP"), A("read:psplit"),
                      S3(A("CONS"), K(A("if")),
                         S3(A("COMP"), A("read:demark_pairs"),
                            S3(A("COMP"), N(2), N(3))))))))))),

DEF("read:derivation_tag",
    S4(A("CONS"), K(A("derivation")),
       S3(A("COMP"), A("read:mode_of"),
          S3(A("COMP"), A("read:marker_of"), A("read:ring_words"))),
       A("read:body_clauses"))),

"THE MARKER DROPPED FROM THE TOKEN PAIRS. read:demark works on plain words; the reader carries <original, Capitalized> pairs, so the same one-or-two decision is made over the pairs' words and the pairs are dropped. Two of the SAME character or one, exactly as read:marker_of decides.",
DEF("read:demark_pairs",
    S4(A("COND"),
       S3(A("COMP"), A("null"),
          S3(A("COMP"), A("read:marker_of"), S2(A("ALPHA"), N(1)))),
       A("id"),
       S3(A("COMP"), A("theta:drop"),
          S3(A("CONS"), A("id"),
             S4(A("COND"),
                S3(A("COMP"), A("eq"),
                   S3(A("CONS"), S3(A("COMP"), N(1), N(1)), S3(A("COMP"), N(1), N(2)))),
                K(N(2)), K(N(1))))))),

"SPLIT A TOKEN-PAIR LIST AT A WORD. read:split compares each element to the needle directly, which works on plain words and never matches when the elements are <original, Capitalized> pairs -- the shape the reader actually carries. This is read:split with one change, comparing the pair's WORD, and it answers <before, after> with the needle dropped.",
DEF("read:psplit",
    S4(A("COMP"),
       S3(A("CONS"), N(2),
          S4(A("COND"), S3(A("COMP"), A("null"), N(3)), K(PHI()),
             S3(A("COMP"), A("tl"), N(3)))),
       S3(A("WHILE"),
          S3(A("COMP"), A("and"),
             S3(A("CONS"),
                S4(A("COMP"), A("not"), A("null"), N(3)),
                S4(A("COMP"), A("not"), A("eq"),
                   S3(A("CONS"),
                      S4(A("COND"), S3(A("COMP"), A("null"), N(3)), K(A("")),
                         S3(A("COMP"), N(1), S3(A("COMP"), N(1), N(3)))),
                      N(1))))),
          S4(A("CONS"), N(1),
             S3(A("COMP"), A("apndr"),
                S3(A("CONS"), N(2), S3(A("COMP"), N(1), N(3)))),
             S3(A("COMP"), A("tl"), N(3)))),
       S4(A("CONS"), N(1), K(PHI()), N(2)))),

"THE BODY'S CLAUSES, split on `and`. Measured over the corpus, 23 of 54 marked rules have a conjunctive body -- 43% -- so a single-clause shortcut would cover half the rules and look like it worked, which is the shape of mistake that costs the most. The fold splits until no `and` remains and appends the tail, so a body with none answers one clause and a body with two answers three.",
DEF("read:clauses",
    S4(A("COMP"),
       S3(A("COMP"), A("apndr"), S3(A("CONS"), N(1), N(2))),
       S3(A("WHILE"),
          S3(A("COMP"), A("read:memberw"),
             S3(A("CONS"), K(A("and")),
                S3(A("COMP"), S2(A("ALPHA"), N(1)), N(2)))),
          S3(A("CONS"),
             S3(A("COMP"), A("apndr"),
                S3(A("CONS"), N(1),
                   S3(A("COMP"), N(1),
                      S3(A("COMP"), A("read:psplit"),
                         S3(A("CONS"), K(A("and")), N(2)))))),
             S3(A("COMP"), N(2),
                S3(A("COMP"), A("read:psplit"),
                   S3(A("CONS"), K(A("and")), N(2)))))),
       S3(A("CONS"), K(PHI()), A("id")))),

"THE HEAD OF A RULE: everything before `if`. Without this the whole sentence became the fact type name -- auto.dev's authorization rule read as one type called *CustomerIsAuthorizedForOperationOnResourceIfCustomerHoldsRoleAndRolePerforms... -- so the rule was RECOGNIZED and not DECOMPOSED. Taking pos1 tokens and dropping the last is the split without arithmetic: firstn includes the `if`, butlast removes it.",
DEF("read:head_pairs",
    S3(A("COMP"), A("theta:butlast"),
       S3(A("COMP"), A("read:firstn"),
          S3(A("CONS"),
             S3(A("COMP"), N(1),
                S3(A("COMP"), A("read:pos1"),
                   S3(A("CONS"), K(A("if")), S2(A("ALPHA"), N(1))))),
             A("id"))))),

DEF("read:derivation_rec",
    S3(A("COMP"), A("read:tag_pair"),
       S3(A("CONS"),
          S3(A("COMP"), A("read:reading"),
             S3(A("CONS"), N(1),
                S3(A("COMP"), A("read:head_pairs"),
                   S3(A("COMP"), A("read:demark_pairs"),
                      S3(A("COMP"), N(2), N(3)))))),
          A("read:derivation_tag")))),

"THE NUMERIC CODES ARE A CONVENTION AND ARE SAID TO BE ONE. The paper fixes almost nothing about HTTP -- one sentence, :110, `A POST /orders request creates an Order and returns its status with the actions available from it` -- and nothing at all about status codes. So this table is a DECLARATION in the sense auth:default_policy is: a decision written down where it can be read and changed, not a derivation dressed up as one. 201 for a commit that added a fact, 200 for a commit carrying reported deontic violations in the representation, 409 for an alethic refusal because the state conflicts with a declared constraint rather than the request being malformed.",
DEF("http:status",
    K(S7(S2(A("committed"), N(201)),
         S2(A("committed_with_violations"), N(200)),
         S2(A("refused"), N(409)),
         S2(A("read"), N(200)),
         S2(A("method_not_admitted"), N(405)),
         S2(A("unknown_resource"), N(404)),
         S2(A("malformed"), N(400))))),

"WHICH ROUTE KIND A METHOD ADDRESSES. Theorem 2 partitions links(e) into transitions (kind i) and nav (kind ii), `disjoint by their operation kind`, and this is the HTTP spelling of that partition -- the only part of the mapping the sources determine. GET reaches a nav route because following one retrieves a peer and changes nothing; POST reaches a transition because :110 has POST creating and a fired trigger fact is what advances a machine. PUT, PATCH and DELETE have no row: the fragment admits no route they name, and an absent row refuses rather than guessing, which is Definition 5 one level up.",
DEF("http:method_kinds",
    K(S4(S2(A("GET"), A("nav")), S2(A("POST"), A("transition")), S2(A("DELETE"), A("retraction")), S2(A("PUT"), A("replacement"))))),

"THE STATUS FOR AN OUTCOME, refusing an outcome it does not name rather than defaulting to 200 -- a serving path that answers OK for something it did not understand is the one failure a client cannot detect.",
DEF("http:status_of",
    S3(A("COMP"),
       S4(A("COND"), A("null"), S2(A("CONST"), PHI()), N(2)),
       S3(A("COMP"), A("theta:find_desc"), S3(A("CONS"), A("id"), A("http:status"))))),

"A TWO-WIDE CELL OUT OF A STORE, by name. system:ftpop_fetch takes <cell, arity> and answers a function of the store; every state-machine cell is two wide, so this is the one shape the links assembly needs. Operand <store, cell>.",
DEF("main:cell2",
    S3(A("COMP"), A("apply"),
       S3(A("CONS"),
          S3(A("COMP"), A("apply"),
             S3(A("CONS"), K(A("system:ftpop_fetch")),
                S3(A("CONS"), N(2), K(N(2))))),
          N(1)))),

"THE TRANSITION TABLE, ASSEMBLED FROM THE STORE. system:links_of wants <from, event, to> and a compiled store holds it in three cells keyed by transition id -- smFrom <tid, from>, smTrigger <tid, event>, smTo <tid, to> -- so the table is two natural joins on that key and a projection dropping it. This is the per-noun spec I earlier called `one composition, not a design question` in fc62f78a, and that was wrong: nothing generates links without first rebuilding this. Measured on real stores -- support.auto.dev carries 44 smFrom, 28 smTrigger, 11 smStatusFt; cancel-service 11, 11, 1.",
"THE MACHINE IS THE METAMODEL IS OWN POPULATIONS. The metamodel supplies the State Machine Definition type so an app can declare its process as facts, and the app populates TransitionIsFromStatus, TransitionIsTriggeredByEventType and TransitionIsToStatus like any other fact type. smFrom, smTrigger and smTo are the ABSTRACT names the sm_join cases feed directly, with no store; a real store has the metamodel ftids. Reading the abstract names here found nothing in every composed store, so main:transitions_of answered the empty table and links(e) was empty for every entity that ever had a machine.",
"AND THE STATUS FACT TYPE IS THE MACHINE IS OBJECT TYPE. A status is a state of an app is machine -- the app declares its statuses and declares the machine for one of its entity types -- so the fact type carrying an entity is current status is that object type is, named the way case status-ft names it: Task_has_Status for Task. smStatusFt is the <object type, status fact type> pairing, and it is derivable from StateMachineDefinitionIsForObjectType rather than being a designation an app has to repeat.",
"AN INITIALIZED ENTITY IS IN THE INITIAL STATUS, which is the + marking rather than a stored fact minted somewhere. An app declares its statuses and declares a machine for one of its entity types; an entity of that type that carries no status row IS in the machine is initial status, and a row appears only when a transition fires. So the status population is SEMI-DERIVED: derivable when absent, directly assertable when a transition writes one, and a stored row always wins because cat puts it first.",
DEF("main:status_ftname",
    S3(A("COMP"), A("implode"),
       S3(A("CONS"), K(A("")), S3(A("CONS"), N(2), K(A("_has_Status")))))),
DEF("main:status_stored",
    S3(A("COMP"), A("system:pop_rows"),
       S3(A("CONS"), A("main:status_ftname"), N(1)))),
DEF("main:status_base",
    S3(A("COMP"), A("cat"),
       S3(A("CONS"),
          A("main:status_stored"),
          S3(A("COMP"), A("distr"),
             S3(A("CONS"),
