"SUBSET PROPOSES, EQUALITY DISPOSES. Samuel: subset is modus ponens and equality is tollens. A subset is forall x (sub x implies super x), so from a value in the sub leg MP infers the super leg -- this message-s number IS some agent-s phone, and the agents holding it are the candidates. That is the whole of match:candidates. An equality carries the REVERSE inclusion as well, and what that buys is the denying direction usably: not-in-B entails not-in-A on the same leg being matched into, so an equality can STRIKE a candidate where a subset can only propose one. Which matters more than it sounds under the singleton rule -- pruning is exactly the operation that turns two candidates into one and makes an otherwise ambiguous match resolve. A subset licenses tollens too, as its own contrapositive, and that is not idle either: backwards it is the CHECK, the refusal when the sub leg holds something the super leg lacks. One implication, two directions, and the direction chosen says whether the constraint is filling or refusing.",
